ufficient?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555476047,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4MzkzNDQwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-483934401"},"message":"Initially, it was direct mode.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555476951,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4MzkzNjg2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-483936860"},"message":"FTR: The code that dives into subdatasets for the sole purpose if figuring out a single \"modified\" label is here: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/master/datalad/support/gitrepo.py#L3260\n\nIndeed, the recent https://github.com/datalad/datalad/pull/3324 might have had a negative impact on the \"full\" evaluation mode. Need to look into that.\n\nOtherwise see https://github.com/datalad/datalad/pull/3324#issue-270052649 for a performance comparison of the different modes on a clean dataset hierarchy.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555513730,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4NDEzMTM0OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-484131349"},"message":"#3343 Adds early stopping to subdataset state evaluation. This is all we can do, I think, until we can tackle #3311.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1555559135,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4NDM0NTA2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-484345060"},"message":"\u003e \u003e I think it might make sense to rely on git status to identify modified datasets instead of manual traversal. Could you remind me why it wasn't sufficient?\n\n\u003e Initially, it was direct mode.\n\nSo, now that there is no direct mode, is there any other reason?\nwhy would we need to reinvent the wheel (ie. not just to use `git status`) and then reinvent caching (#3311) which is already done by git itself for `git status`?  \nI am just wondering -- may be there is something we do which git doesn't?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555560773,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4NDM0ODk1MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-484348950"},"message":"We are using a variety of queries, starting lower than what `git status` does. Previous attempts to build a system around `git status` failed. One reason is that its internal recursion can only be prevented for the price of not getting any report on submodules. We are reinventing, because we need a solution that fits all demands, not just one aspect.\n\nThat being said","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555560773,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIxMTQ5NDY3MA=="},"target":"e155614aca0932422038471c741151eae75cd4d6ff70fe953817650a8a5f5504","message":"We are using a variety of queries, starting lower than what `git status` does. Previous attempts to build a system around `git status` failed. One reason is that its internal recursion can only be prevented for the price of not getting any report on submodules. We are reinventing, because we need a solution that fits all demands, not just one aspect.\n\nThat being said, I would be happy to review a PR that does all this, is comprehensible, and still overall faster than what we have.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555560666,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjI4NDU2MTM0Mg=="},"status":2},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555560669,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEzOlJlb3BlbmVkRXZlbnQyMjg0NTYxNDA2"},"status":1},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555560845,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjI4NDU2NDMyMg=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558717852,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5NTcxMzk5Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-495713997"},"message":"caching might be a solution, or not... dunno. https://github.com/datalad/datalad/commit/8bf73757960f9c251ab7ad6b44e8eee59d304625 did improve things but  IMHO the issue remains (thus reopening).  Here is another sample\n```shell\n$ time git status\nOn branch master\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\n\nreal    0m0.574s\nuser    0m0.420s\nsys     0m0.760s\n\n$ time datalad status\n\nreal    5m32.566s\nuser    5m23.564s\nsys     0m15.064s\n```\nin a heavy dataset/repository with ~1500 sub-directories on the top level, altogether `ls-tree -r` lists 235270 files/directories. All clean.\nWanted to use `datalad run`, but it seems that the only sensible way would be just with --explicit to avoid overall status check wait (related: #3074 demanding --explicit for rerun)\nobservation -- `git cat-file --batch` and `datalad status` seems to work nicely in tandem but \"serially\" consuming ~75% and ~25% CPU accordingly (so no silly sleeps)... probably largely just to \"communicate\" on 200k files","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558717852,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEzOlJlb3BlbmVkRXZlbnQyMzY2MTY5MzM0"},"status":1},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1558718151,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5NTcxNTkwNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-495715906"},"message":"It seems it would be sensible to turn of file type detection in run.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1558730214,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5NTc4MTEwMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-495781103"},"message":"\u003e It seems it would be sensible to turn of file type detection in run.\n\nI'm not sure what that would entail, but IMO GitRepo.dirty, which is what `run` is using, doesn't need to use `status()` underneath.  It takes no parameters and returns a boolean, so something along the lines of `\u003cgit status --porcelain\u003e.strip()` would suffice.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558733873,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5NTc5NTAwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-495795001"},"message":"Inspired but a just arrived newbie confusion report about `unsaved modifications present`... Ideally, the assessment of dirtiness and message about unstaged changes should provide some (at least a single file and its status) information about what is changed.  \n\n E.g. `[unsaved modifications (deleted blah, possibly more) present, save the changes or run in --explicit mode]`\n\nAny command which runs underneath for `is_dirty` check actually receives information about that (even if exits early), so theoretically it should be possible.  Again -- this is an IMHO ideal informative message. Sure thing we could just to tell user to run `datalad status` command to discover what has changed ;-)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1558853541,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5NTk3NDY2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-495974665"},"message":"\u003e I'm not sure what that would entail, but IMO GitRepo.dirty, which is what run is using, doesn't need to use status() underneath. It takes no parameters and returns a boolean, so something along the lines of \u003cgit status --porcelain\u003e.strip() would suffice.\n\nI think this is a great idea, and I should have done that to begin with (but I was in the mind set of replacing code with uniform pieces).\n\n\u003e Inspired but a just arrived newbie confusion report about unsaved modifications present... Ideally, the assessment of dirtiness and message about unstaged changes should provide some (at least a single file and its status) information about what is changed.\n\nThis, unfortunately, directly conflicts with the desire for more speed. I'd prefer to combine @kyleam approach with the second part of your proposal:\n\n\u003e Sure thing we could just to tell user to run datalad status command to discover what has changed\n\nOtherwise we would always pay the price of thorough status evaluation, just for the benefit of being able to deliver an optimal error message fast -- but we should not optimize for fast errors ;-)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1558899569,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5NjAyNTgyNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-496025826"},"message":"\u003e  I'd prefer to combine @kyleam approach with the second part of your proposal:\n\nI've done that in #3460.\n\n\u003e Otherwise we would always pay the price of thorough status evaluation, just for the benefit of being able to deliver an optimal error message fast -- but we should not optimize for fast errors ;-)\n\nI agree, though in my eyes an error message with the status information folded in isn't even an optimal error message.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1559159322,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5NzA4MzY2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-497083665"},"message":"\u003e \u003e Inspired but a just arrived newbie confusion report about unsaved modifications present... Ideally, the assessment of dirtiness and message about unstaged changes should provide some (at least a single file and its status) information about what is changed.\n\u003e \n\u003e This, unfortunately, directly conflicts with the desire for more speed. I'd prefer to combine @kyleam approach with the second part of your proposal:\n\nnot really, e.g. implementation in #3460 is\n```python\nreturn bool(stdout.strip())\n```\nso `stdout` (output of `git status`) is captured and then discarded just to return a `bool` value.  What I was hinting for is to have a function like `a_dirty_thing` (name could be better ;)) which returns `None` (or another False'y thing, you decide) if there is nothing dirty, and returns first (or a list of items up to some N) dirty items which an then be listed in the message.  So performance wise it would be the same (the same call), but UI would be able to take advantage by providing more informative message.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1564848423,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUxNzkzNTY4OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-517935689"},"message":"Saving in a sizeable repo (380k files) with 13k files being `git rm`ed already (interrupted `remove` due to #3441 -- didn't have another hour to wait ) takes \u003e14 minutes . process was \"busy\" for a while talking to `git cat-file --batch` but I stopped eye balling it.\n\n`git status` took 2 sec to report all removed files to be committed.  Didn't check how long it would be just to just `git commit` since either `datalad save` or it  and I decided to time `save`\n\ndatalad: 0.12.0rc4-311-gf833c282","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578685582,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3MzE3OTQ0Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3342#issuecomment-573179446"},"message":"I think with #3701 being merged months ago, this issue can be closed. There is a related one for `diff` #3990","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578685582,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjkzOTY2Mzk2OA=="},"status":2}]}